  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Thursday)\nIssued at 1134 AM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nHigh pressure will continue to dominate the forecast with low \nimpact weather likely to persist through this weekend. The only \nexception may be across NE OK on Friday, where limited fire \nweather conditions could develop during the afternoon. Winds will \nbe higher Friday, especially along and NW of I-44, and with min \nRHs in the 25 to 35 percent range, locally enhanced grassland fire\nspread rates are forecast. Temperatures will remain well above \naverage for the next several days with highs near or above 80 \ndegrees.\n\nBy late Sunday, a potent upper low will begin to navigate E-NE \nout of the desert southwest, eventually moving into the C Plains \nMonday. This system will interact with a frontal boundary and \nproduce increasing precipitation to our north and west. Some of \nthis may move into at least parts of E OK Monday and Tuesday, but \nPoPs generally remain less than 20 percent at this time. Models \ndiverge beyond this point and disagree on how to handle the \naforementioned frontal boundary. Some solutions indicate the \nfront will push south into the forecast area and help to clear \nthings out, while other solutions keep the boundary to our north. \nThe latest NBM seems to favor the former and brings cooler \ntemperatures into the area Tuesday. Despite higher variability \nearly in the week, guidance does appear to be in reasonable \nagreement that a strong trough will swing through the C/N Plains \nWed-Thu. This would likely send a stronger front into our area \nwith additional rain potential and another chance for cooler \ntemperatures.",
